<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en-GB">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Unit 16 Progressive Test</title>
<style>
:root{
  --bg:#f4f5f8; --panel:#ffffff; --ink:#15181f; --muted:#5a6376; --line:#e2e5ec;
  --accent:#3b4ea0; --accent-soft:#eef0fa; --ok:#1c7a4a; --ok-soft:#e6f4ec;
  --bad:#b4262c; --bad-soft:#fbeaea; --warn:#8a5a00; --warn-soft:#fdf2dc;
  --l1:#3b4ea0; --l2:#1c7a7a; --l3:#8a5a00; --l4:#8a3a7a; --l5:#b4262c;
  --radius:10px; --shadow:0 1px 2px rgba(20,24,40,.06),0 6px 20px rgba(20,24,40,.06);
}
@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){
  :root:not([data-theme="light"]){
    --bg:#111318; --panel:#1a1d25; --ink:#e9ecf3; --muted:#9aa3b6; --line:#2b303b;
    --accent:#8ea0e8; --accent-soft:#232842; --ok:#6ed19b; --ok-soft:#182a20;
    --bad:#ef8a8a; --bad-soft:#2c1a1c; --warn:#e5b95e; --warn-soft:#2d2513;
    --l1:#8ea0e8; --l2:#6fc9c9; --l3:#e5b95e; --l4:#d79ad0; --l5:#ef8a8a;
    --shadow:0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.4),0 6px 20px rgba(0,0,0,.3);
  }
}
*{box-sizing:border-box}
body{
  margin:0; background:var(--bg); color:var(--ink);
  font:16px/1.6 -apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",Roboto,"Helvetica Neue",Arial,sans-serif;
  -webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased;
}
.wrap{max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 16px 96px}

/* ---------- header ---------- */
header.top{
  background:var(--panel); border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);
  position:sticky; top:0; z-index:40; box-shadow:0 1px 0 var(--line);
}
.top-inner{max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:12px 16px;display:flex;gap:16px;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap}
.brand{font-weight:700;font-size:15px;letter-spacing:-.01em;flex:1 1 220px}
.brand small{display:block;font-weight:500;color:var(--muted);font-size:12.5px;letter-spacing:0}
.score-chip{
  background:var(--accent-soft); color:var(--accent); border-radius:999px;
  padding:6px 14px; font-weight:700; font-size:14px; white-space:nowrap;
}
.bar{height:4px;background:var(--line);border-radius:999px;overflow:hidden;width:100%;margin-top:4px}
.bar>i{display:block;height:100%;background:var(--accent);width:0;transition:width .4s ease}

/* ---------- level nav ---------- */
nav.levels{display:flex;gap:6px;overflow-x:auto;padding:10px 16px;max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;scrollbar-width:thin}
nav.levels button{
  flex:0 0 auto; border:1px solid var(--line); background:var(--panel); color:var(--muted);
  padding:7px 13px; border-radius:999px; font:600 13px/1 inherit; cursor:pointer; white-space:nowrap;
}
nav.levels button[aria-current="true"]{background:var(--accent);border-color:var(--accent);color:#fff}
nav.levels button .tick{color:var(--ok);font-weight:800}
nav.levels button[aria-current="true"] .tick{color:#fff}

/* ---------- cards ---------- */
.card{background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--radius);box-shadow:var(--shadow);padding:22px;margin:16px 0}
.level-head{padding:26px 22px;border-left:5px solid var(--accent)}
.level-head h2{margin:0 0 4px;font-size:22px;letter-spacing:-.02em}
.level-head p{margin:0;color:var(--muted);font-size:14.5px}
.level-meta{margin-top:14px;display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.tag{font:600 11.5px/1 inherit;letter-spacing:.03em;text-transform:uppercase;padding:5px 9px;border-radius:5px;background:var(--accent-soft);color:var(--accent)}
.tag.grey{background:var(--bg);color:var(--muted);border:1px solid var(--line)}

.q{border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding:22px 0 4px}
.q:first-of-type{border-top:0;padding-top:4px}
.q-head{display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:baseline;margin-bottom:10px}
.q-num{font:700 13px/1.5 inherit;color:var(--muted);flex:0 0 auto;min-width:34px}
.q-text{font-weight:600;flex:1}
.q-marks{font:600 12px/1.5 inherit;color:var(--muted);white-space:nowrap}
.q-ref{font:500 11.5px/1 inherit;color:var(--muted);letter-spacing:.02em;margin-left:44px;margin-bottom:10px;text-transform:uppercase}

.scenario{background:var(--accent-soft);border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;margin:0 0 16px;font-size:14.5px}
.scenario > b:first-child{display:block;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.05em;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--accent);margin-bottom:5px}
.scenario b{font-weight:700}

.teach code,.scenario code,.model code,.q-text code{font:13px/1.5 ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:4px;padding:1px 5px;word-break:break-word}
.scenario code{display:inline-block;margin:1px 0}
table.matrix{border-collapse:collapse;margin:8px 0 12px;font-size:14px;width:100%;max-width:520px}
table.matrix th,table.matrix td{border:1px solid var(--line);padding:8px 10px;text-align:center}
table.matrix th{background:var(--bg);font-weight:600}
table.matrix td.l{background:var(--ok-soft);color:var(--ok);font-weight:700}
table.matrix td.m{background:var(--warn-soft);color:var(--warn);font-weight:700}
table.matrix td.h{background:var(--bad-soft);color:var(--bad);font-weight:700}
table.matrix td.x{background:var(--bad);color:#fff;font-weight:800}
/* ---------- knowledge recap ---------- */
.recap{border-left:5px solid var(--ok)}
.recap h3{margin:0 0 4px;font-size:17px;letter-spacing:-.01em}
.recap>p.lead{margin:0 0 16px;color:var(--muted);font-size:14px}
.recap-item{border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding:14px 0}
.recap-item:first-of-type{border-top:0;padding-top:0}
.recap-item h4{margin:0 0 6px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--ok)}
.recap-item p{margin:0 0 8px;font-size:14.5px}
.recap-item p:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
.recap-item ul{margin:6px 0 0;padding-left:20px;font-size:14.5px}
.recap-item li{margin-bottom:4px}
.recap-item .kw{font-weight:600}
.recap-toggle{margin-top:6px}

.teach{
  margin:0 0 12px 44px;padding:11px 14px;border-radius:8px;
  background:var(--warn-soft);border-left:3px solid var(--warn);font-size:14px
}
.teach > b:first-child{display:block;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.05em;font-size:11px;color:var(--warn);margin-bottom:4px}
.teach b{font-weight:700}
@media(max-width:640px){.teach,.opts,.rows,.written,.q-ref,.feedback{margin-left:0!important}}

/* ---------- options ---------- */
.opts{margin-left:44px;display:grid;gap:7px}
.opt{display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:flex-start;padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;cursor:pointer;font-size:15px;background:var(--panel)}
.opt:hover{border-color:var(--accent)}
.opt input{margin:4px 0 0;flex:0 0 auto;accent-color:var(--accent)}
.opt.correct{background:var(--ok-soft);border-color:var(--ok)}
.opt.wrong{background:var(--bad-soft);border-color:var(--bad)}
.opt.missed{border-color:var(--ok);border-style:dashed}

/* ---------- match / classify ---------- */
.rows{margin-left:44px;display:grid;gap:8px}
.row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 210px;gap:10px;align-items:center;padding:10px 12px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;font-size:14.5px}
.row select{font:inherit;font-size:14px;padding:7px 8px;border-radius:6px;border:1px solid var(--line);background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink);width:100%}
.row.correct{background:var(--ok-soft);border-color:var(--ok)}
.row.wrong{background:var(--bad-soft);border-color:var(--bad)}
.row .fix{grid-column:1/-1;font-size:13px;color:var(--bad);font-weight:600}
@media(max-width:640px){.row{grid-template-columns:1fr}}

/* ---------- written ---------- */
.written{margin-left:44px}
textarea{
  width:100%;min-height:130px;font:inherit;font-size:15px;padding:12px;border-radius:8px;
  border:1px solid var(--line);background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink);resize:vertical
}
textarea:focus{outline:2px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:1px}
.hint{font-size:13.5px;color:var(--muted);margin:8px 0 0}
.hint b{color:var(--ink)}

.model{margin-top:14px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-left:4px solid var(--ok);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;background:var(--ok-soft);display:none}
.model.show{display:block}
.model h4{margin:0 0 8px;font-size:13px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--ok)}
.model p{margin:0 0 10px;font-size:14.5px}
.model p:last-child{margin-bottom:0}

.criteria{margin-top:14px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;padding:16px;display:none}
.criteria.show{display:block}
.criteria h4{margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted)}
.criteria .sub{font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);margin:0 0 12px}
.crit{display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:flex-start;padding:7px 0;font-size:14.5px;cursor:pointer;border-top:1px solid var(--line)}
.crit:first-of-type{border-top:0}
.crit input{margin:4px 0 0;accent-color:var(--ok)}
.self-score{margin-top:12px;padding-top:12px;border-top:2px solid var(--line);font-weight:700;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.self-score span.val{color:var(--accent);font-size:18px}

/* ---------- buttons ---------- */
button.btn{
  font:600 14px/1 inherit;padding:11px 18px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--accent);
  background:var(--accent);color:#fff;cursor:pointer
}
button.btn.ghost{background:transparent;color:var(--accent)}
button.btn.grey{background:transparent;color:var(--muted);border-color:var(--line)}
button.btn:disabled{opacity:.5;cursor:default}
.actions{display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:22px;padding-top:20px;border-top:1px solid var(--line)}

.feedback{margin-left:44px;margin-top:10px;font-size:14px;padding:10px 12px;border-radius:7px;display:none}
.feedback.show{display:block}
.feedback.good{background:var(--ok-soft);color:var(--ok)}
.feedback.bad{background:var(--bad-soft);color:var(--bad)}
.feedback b{display:block;margin-bottom:3px}

.level-result{margin-top:18px;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;background:var(--accent-soft);display:none;font-size:15px}
.level-result.show{display:block}
.level-result strong{font-size:20px}

/* ---------- results ---------- */
.big-score{text-align:center;padding:10px 0 20px}
.big-score .pct{font-size:56px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-.03em;line-height:1}
.big-score .of{color:var(--muted);font-size:15px;margin-top:6px}
.grade{display:inline-block;margin-top:14px;padding:8px 20px;border-radius:999px;font-weight:700;font-size:15px}
.grade.d{background:var(--ok-soft);color:var(--ok)}
.grade.m{background:var(--accent-soft);color:var(--accent)}
.grade.p{background:var(--warn-soft);color:var(--warn)}
.grade.u{background:var(--bad-soft);color:var(--bad)}
table.bd{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;font-size:14.5px}
table.bd th,table.bd td{text-align:left;padding:9px 6px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}
table.bd th{font-size:12px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.04em;color:var(--muted)}
table.bd td.n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;font-weight:600}
.meter{height:7px;background:var(--line);border-radius:999px;overflow:hidden;min-width:90px}
.meter>i{display:block;height:100%;background:var(--accent)}
ul.revise{margin:8px 0 0;padding-left:20px;font-size:14.5px}
ul.revise li{margin-bottom:6px}

.hidden{display:none}
@media print{
  header.top,nav.levels,.actions,button{display:none!important}
  .card{break-inside:avoid;box-shadow:none}
  body{background:#fff}
}
.srcs{font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);margin:28px 0 40px;line-height:1.55}
.srcs a{color:var(--accent)}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header class="top">
  <div class="top-inner">
    <div class="brand">Unit 16: Cloud Storage and Collaboration Tools — Progressive Test
      <small>BTEC Level 3 National IT · Learning aims A, B and C</small>
    </div>
    <div class="score-chip" id="chip">0 / 0 marks</div>
  </div>
  <div style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 16px 8px"><div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div></div>
  <nav class="levels" id="nav"></nav>
</header>

<div class="wrap">
  <main id="main"></main>
  <footer class="srcs">
    <p><b>Built from:</b> <a href="https://qualifications.pearson.com/content/dam/pdf/BTEC-Nationals/Information-Technology/2016/specification-and-sample-assessments/specification-pearson-btec-level-3-national-extended-diploma-in-information-technology.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pearson BTEC Level 3 National Extended Diploma in IT specification, Unit 16</a> (also an optional unit in the Diploma; not available in the Extended Certificate).</p>
    <p><b>Case evidence:</b> <a href="https://www.computerweekly.com/news/366633536/AWS-apologises-for-14-hour-outage-and-sets-out-causes-of-US-datacentre-region-downtime" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Computer Weekly on the AWS outage</a> · <a href="https://azure.status.microsoft/en-us/status/history/?trackingId=YKYN-BWZ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Front Door incident review</a> · <a href="https://btw.media/en/the-ovhcloud-fire-showed-that-data-locality-is-not-disaster-separation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OVHcloud fire analysis</a> · <a href="https://www.theregister.com/off-prem/2025/07/25/microsoft-exec-admits-it-cannot-guarantee-data-sovereignty/458553" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Register on Microsoft France</a> · <a href="https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/688b20e6ff8c05468cb7b120/summary_of_final_decision.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CMA cloud final decision</a> · <a href="https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/legacy/sp/nistspecialpublication800-145.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIST SP 800-145</a> · <a href="https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/international-transfers/a-brief-guide-to-international-transfers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ICO international transfers</a></p>
  </footer>
</div>

<script>
/* UNIT 16 — PROGRESSIVE TEST · Cloud Storage and Collaboration Tools */
const SCEN = "<p><b>Harbour &amp; Lane Architects</b> is a 38-person practice with a main studio on Newcastle Quayside and a smaller office in Durham. Site surveyors spend two or three days a week on building sites using tablets. All files sit on a single Windows Server 2016 file server in a store cupboard in Newcastle; the office manager takes a nightly USB backup home. Email runs on an ageing on-premises Exchange 2016 server. Drawing files from the CAD package are typically 200 MB to 2 GB. Drawings are sent to clients as email attachments or on memory sticks, and Durham staff connect to Newcastle over a slow VPN. Six staff work part-time or compressed hours, and one designer is visually impaired and uses a screen reader. IT is looked after by the office manager plus an external contractor who visits once a month. Broadband is 80 Mbps down and 20 Mbps up in Newcastle, and 40 Mbps down and 10 Mbps up in Durham. The practice holds client names, addresses and some planning-related personal data. The partners have asked for a cloud storage and collaboration solution, but they are worried about cost, security and what happens if the internet goes down.</p>";

const DATA = [
/* ========================= LEVEL 1 ========================= */
{
  id:"L1", name:"Level 1", colour:"var(--l1)",
  title:"Level 1 · Knowledge and Recall",
  blurb:"Straight recall of the reasons for cloud adoption, the five key characteristics, service and delivery models, the legal framework, and the vocabulary of planning, testing and reviewing a cloud solution. Technical language used precisely is what turns a Pass explanation into a Merit comparison.",
  meta:["Learning aims A–C","1 mark per question"],
  recap:[
    {h:"How this test works",
     b:"<p>Five levels, rising in difficulty. <span class='kw'>Levels 1 and 2</span> mark themselves: answer, then press <em>Check this level</em> for your mark and an explanation of every answer. <span class='kw'>Levels 3, 4 and 5</span> are written: type your answer first, then reveal the model answer and mark scheme, and tick only the points you actually made. Your running total sits top right; the <em>Results</em> tab breaks it down by learning aim and by assessment criterion and tells you what to revise.</p><p>Read the recap at the start of each level before answering. Nothing is saved when you close the page, so finish a level in one sitting or print your results at the end.</p>"},
    {h:"The assessment",
     b:"<p>Unit 16 is <span class='kw'>internally assessed</span>: 60 guided learning hours, a maximum of <span class='kw'>two assignments</span>, set and marked by your centre and quality-assured by Pearson. <span class='kw'>Assignment 1 (learning aim A)</span>: A.P1, A.P2, A.M1, A.D1, using a <span class='kw'>real organisation with no or limited cloud adoption</span>. <span class='kw'>Assignment 2 (learning aims B and C)</span>: B.P3, B.P4, C.P5, C.P6, B.M2, C.M3, BC.D2, BC.D3. You design, build, test and manage a working solution for at least <span class='kw'>two weeks</span>. The marks in this test are for practice only; your real grade comes from the criteria.</p>"},
    {h:"A1 Cloud computing in organisations",
     b:"<p>Reasons organisations adopt cloud: <span class='kw'>improved productivity</span>, <span class='kw'>improved or diversified service</span>, <span class='kw'>improved user experience</span>, <span class='kw'>reduced costs</span> and <span class='kw'>flexibility</span>.</p><ul><li><span class='kw'>On-demand self-service</span>: the customer provisions storage, users or servers through a portal with no human at the provider involved.</li><li><span class='kw'>Ubiquitous access</span>: available over the network from any location on standard devices (browser, phone, tablet).</li><li><span class='kw'>Resource pooling</span>: the provider's physical resources serve many customers at once, allocated dynamically (multi-tenancy).</li><li><span class='kw'>Measured service</span>: usage is metered, so you pay for what you use and can see what each department consumes.</li><li><span class='kw'>Rapid elasticity</span>: capacity scales up or down quickly, sometimes automatically, to match demand.</li></ul><p>These five match the NIST definition (SP 800-145) that most textbooks are based on.</p>"},
    {h:"A2 Models",
     b:"<ul><li><span class='kw'>SaaS</span>: finished software delivered over the internet (Microsoft 365, Google Workspace, Dropbox). The provider manages everything except your data, users and settings.</li><li><span class='kw'>PaaS</span>: a managed platform to build and run your own applications (Azure App Service, Google App Engine). You manage the code and data.</li><li><span class='kw'>IaaS</span>: virtual machines, storage and networks (Amazon EC2, Azure Virtual Machines). You manage the guest operating system upwards.</li></ul><p><span class='kw'>Virtualisation</span> is what makes cloud possible: a hypervisor splits one physical server into many isolated virtual machines, which enables resource pooling, elasticity and multi-tenancy.</p><p>Delivery models: <span class='kw'>public</span> (shared, provider-owned), <span class='kw'>private</span> (one organisation), <span class='kw'>hybrid</span> (a mix, connected), <span class='kw'>community</span> (shared by organisations with common concerns, such as a group of councils or schools). Hosting can be <span class='kw'>self-hosted</span> or <span class='kw'>third-party</span>. Key terms: <span class='kw'>automation</span>, <span class='kw'>cloud bursting</span> (overflowing from private to public cloud at peak), <span class='kw'>just-in-time service</span>, <span class='kw'>multi-tenancy</span>.</p>"},
    {h:"A3–A5 Requirements, impact and law",
     b:"<p><span class='kw'>A3 requirements</span>: current IT systems and services, organisational aims, customers, staff (job role, accessibility, working patterns), location, legal and ethical (licensing, data security, privacy) and requirements cloud cannot meet, such as controlling machinery on a factory floor.</p><p><span class='kw'>A4 impact</span>: on individuals (flexibility, cost, efficiency, risks such as data availability and third-party ownership, challenges such as support, skills and training) and on organisations (user experience, staffing, cost including <span class='kw'>pay-as-you-grow</span> and <span class='kw'>chargeback</span>, implementation, backup and disaster recovery, constraints such as bandwidth and firewall restrictions, legal ownership and jurisdiction, dispute resolution).</p><p><span class='kw'>A5 legislation</span>: Data Protection Act 2018 and UK GDPR (amended by the Data (Use and Access) Act 2025), Computer Misuse Act 1990, Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988, telecommunications lawful business practice and interception rules, Fraud Act 2006, legal liability and contractual obligations.</p>"},
    {h:"B and C Plan, build, test, review",
     b:"<p><span class='kw'>B1</span> plan: purpose, client requirements, user needs, technical requirements, legal and ethical issues, risks and mitigation. <span class='kw'>B2</span> review designs with the client, document meetings, refine and update the specification. <span class='kw'>B3</span> develop: infrastructure (server, OS, virtual instance), platform (guest OS image, host resource allocation), software, <span class='kw'>user management</span> (new users, passwords, storage quotas, privileges, access rights), cloud clients (browser plug-ins, mobile apps), managing collaboration (targets, protocols, roles), documentation.</p><p><span class='kw'>C1</span> testing: <span class='kw'>functional, user, performance, stress, security</span>. <span class='kw'>C2</span> reviewing: security patches and breaches, software updates, compatibility, changing requirements, hardware developments. <span class='kw'>C3</span> lessons learned. <span class='kw'>C4</span> presentation skills. <span class='kw'>C5</span> reviewing your own skills, knowledge and behaviours.</p>"}
  ],
  questions:[
  {type:"mcq", area:"A", ao:"Unit overview", ref:"Specification · Assessment",
   q:"How is Unit 16 assessed?",
   options:["A 2-hour written exam set and marked by Pearson","Internally, through a maximum of two assignments set and marked by the centre","A supervised 5-hour practical task set by Pearson","Internally, through four short assignments, one per learning aim"],
   answer:1,
   explain:"Unit 16 is internal. The specification allows a maximum of two assignments: one for learning aim A, and one combining learning aims B and C."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A1 characteristics",
   q:"A team leader creates a new shared workspace and adds 200 GB of storage through a web portal at 9pm, without contacting anyone at the provider. Which key characteristic is this?",
   options:["Resource pooling","Measured service","On-demand self-service","Ubiquitous access"],
   answer:2,
   explain:"On-demand self-service means the customer provisions resources themselves, when they want, without human interaction with the provider."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A1 characteristics",
   q:"Which statement best describes rapid elasticity?",
   options:["Data is copied to several data centres for resilience","Capacity can be scaled up and released quickly, often automatically, to match demand","The provider charges only for what is used","Users can reach the service from any device with an internet connection"],
   answer:1,
   explain:"Elasticity is about scaling capacity up and down quickly. Option C is measured service; option D is ubiquitous access; option A is redundancy, which is a benefit but not one of the five characteristics."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A1 characteristics",
   q:"A provider's physical servers in one data centre host the files of thousands of separate customers, with each customer's data isolated from the others. Which characteristic does this show?",
   options:["Resource pooling","Rapid elasticity","On-demand self-service","Cloud bursting"],
   answer:0,
   explain:"Resource pooling: the provider's resources serve many customers (multi-tenancy) and are allocated dynamically. Virtualisation is the technology that makes it work."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A2 service models",
   q:"Which of these is the clearest example of Platform as a Service (PaaS)?",
   options:["Google Workspace","Renting a Windows Server virtual machine on Amazon EC2","Deploying your own web app code to Azure App Service, which manages the runtime and operating system","Buying a NAS drive for the office"],
   answer:2,
   explain:"PaaS gives you a managed platform: you bring code and data, the provider runs the OS and runtime. Google Workspace is SaaS; an EC2 virtual machine is IaaS; a NAS is on-premises."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A2 virtualisation",
   q:"What is the link between virtualisation and cloud computing?",
   options:["Virtualisation is a type of SaaS","A hypervisor lets one physical server run many isolated virtual machines, which enables resource pooling and elasticity","Virtualisation is only used in private clouds","Virtualisation removes the need for physical data centres"],
   answer:1,
   explain:"Without virtualisation, providers could not split hardware between tenants or spin capacity up in seconds. It underpins IaaS directly and PaaS and SaaS indirectly."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A2 key terms",
   q:"A university runs its student records system in its own private cloud. During clearing week, extra demand automatically overflows to a public cloud provider. What is this called?",
   options:["Multi-tenancy","Just-in-time service","Cloud bursting","Chargeback"],
   answer:2,
   explain:"Cloud bursting: a private (or on-premises) environment bursts into public cloud capacity when it reaches its limit. It is a typical use of a hybrid delivery model."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A2 delivery models",
   q:"Five neighbouring councils share one cloud platform that is built to meet public-sector security requirements and is closed to other customers. Which delivery model is this?",
   options:["Public","Private","Hybrid","Community"],
   answer:3,
   explain:"A community cloud is shared by several organisations with common concerns (security, compliance, mission), but is not open to the general public."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"A", ao:"A.M1", ref:"Learning aim A · A4 cost",
   q:"The finance team at a college is billed internally each month for the exact cloud storage its staff used. What is this practice called?",
   options:["Pay-as-you-grow","Chargeback","Capital expenditure","Measured elasticity"],
   answer:1,
   explain:"Chargeback allocates cloud costs back to the departments that used them. It depends on measured service. Pay-as-you-grow describes how the organisation as a whole pays the provider."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"A", ao:"A.P1", ref:"Learning aim A · A3 requirements",
   q:"Which requirement is a cloud service least able to meet on its own?",
   options:["Letting staff share documents between two offices","Running real-time control of CNC machines on a factory floor","Backing up email off-site","Providing video calls for remote workers"],
   answer:1,
   explain:"The specification names manufacturing as an example of requirements cloud cannot meet. Real-time machine control needs local, low-latency systems that keep running if the internet link fails."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A5 legislation",
   q:"A former employee uses a colleague's saved password to log in to the company's cloud drive and download files. Which Act is most directly broken?",
   options:["Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988","Computer Misuse Act 1990","Fraud Act 2006","Data (Use and Access) Act 2025"],
   answer:1,
   explain:"Unauthorised access to computer material is the first Computer Misuse Act offence. If the files contain personal data, the organisation may also have UK GDPR duties, but the offence by the individual is under the CMA."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"C", ao:"C.P5", ref:"Learning aim C · C1 testing",
   q:"You push 150 simultaneous uploads of 1 GB files at a shared folder to find the point where the service fails. Which type of test is this?",
   options:["Functional testing","User testing","Stress testing","Security testing"],
   answer:2,
   explain:"Stress testing goes beyond normal load to find the breaking point. Performance testing checks speed under expected load; functional testing checks features work as specified."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"B", ao:"B.P4", ref:"Learning aim B · B3 user management",
   q:"Which of these is part of user management in B3?",
   options:["Installing a browser plug-in","Choosing a guest OS image","Setting a 50 GB storage quota for each designer","Allocating host RAM to a virtual instance"],
   answer:2,
   explain:"B3 user management covers new users, passwords, storage quotas, privileges and access rights. Plug-ins are cloud clients; OS images and host resource allocation are platform tasks."},
  {type:"mcq", area:"C", ao:"C.P6", ref:"Learning aim C · C2 reviewing",
   q:"After a fortnight, the client says staff on old iPads cannot open the new collaboration app. Which C2 review area does this fall under?",
   options:["Hardware developments","Compatibility of operating systems and browsers or plug-ins","Security breaches","Stress testing"],
   answer:1,
   explain:"Compatibility with operating systems, browsers and plug-ins is a named C2 area. The fix might be a minimum OS version, or a browser-based fallback."}
  ]
},

/* ========================= LEVEL 2 ========================= */
{
  id:"L2", name:"Level 2", colour:"var(--l2)",
  title:"Level 2 · Classify and Connect",
  blurb:"Sort, match and connect. Can you tell the five characteristics apart in real situations, place products in the right service model, pick the right delivery model and apply the right law? Each item is one mark.",
  meta:["Learning aims A–C","1 mark per item"],
  recap:[
    {h:"Shared responsibility",
     b:"<p>Whatever the service model, the customer is always responsible for its <span class='kw'>data, user accounts and access settings</span>. The higher up the stack you buy, the more the provider manages.</p><table class='matrix'><tr><th>Layer</th><th>On-premises</th><th>IaaS</th><th>PaaS</th><th>SaaS</th></tr><tr><td>Data, users, access</td><td>You</td><td>You</td><td>You</td><td>You</td></tr><tr><td>Application</td><td>You</td><td>You</td><td>You</td><td>Provider</td></tr><tr><td>Runtime and middleware</td><td>You</td><td>You</td><td>Provider</td><td>Provider</td></tr><tr><td>Guest operating system</td><td>You</td><td>You</td><td>Provider</td><td>Provider</td></tr><tr><td>Virtualisation, servers, storage, network</td><td>You</td><td>Provider</td><td>Provider</td><td>Provider</td></tr><tr><td>Physical data centre</td><td>You</td><td>Provider</td><td>Provider</td><td>Provider</td></tr></table>"},
    {h:"Choosing a delivery model",
     b:"<ul><li><span class='kw'>Public</span>: lowest entry cost, fastest to start, most elastic, least control.</li><li><span class='kw'>Private</span>: most control and customisation, highest cost; suits strict regulation or legacy systems.</li><li><span class='kw'>Hybrid</span>: sensitive or latency-critical work stays private, everything else goes public; supports cloud bursting.</li><li><span class='kw'>Community</span>: shared cost between organisations with the same compliance needs.</li></ul>"},
    {h:"The law in one line each",
     b:"<ul><li><span class='kw'>DPA 2018 / UK GDPR</span>: process personal data lawfully and securely; restricted transfers outside the UK need adequacy or safeguards; report qualifying breaches to the ICO within 72 hours.</li><li><span class='kw'>Computer Misuse Act 1990</span>: unauthorised access; access with intent; unauthorised acts that impair a computer.</li><li><span class='kw'>Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988</span>: protects software, drawings and documents; software licences must be respected in the cloud too.</li><li><span class='kw'>Telecommunications lawful business practice</span>: businesses may monitor or record communications on their systems for legitimate reasons if users are told. The original 2000 Regulations were replaced in 2018 by regulations under the Investigatory Powers Act 2016.</li><li><span class='kw'>Fraud Act 2006</span>: false representation, failing to disclose, abuse of position.</li><li><span class='kw'>Contractual obligations</span>: the service level agreement (SLA) and terms decide uptime, liability, data location and exit.</li></ul>"}
  ],
  questions:[
  {type:"classify", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A1 characteristics",
   q:"Which key characteristic of cloud computing does each situation show?",
   options:["On-demand self-service","Ubiquitous access","Resource pooling","Measured service","Rapid elasticity"],
   items:[
    {text:"A surveyor opens the latest drawing on a tablet from a building site in Gateshead", answer:"Ubiquitous access"},
    {text:"The monthly invoice lists storage used in GB and the number of active licences", answer:"Measured service"},
    {text:"An online retailer's servers double automatically on Black Friday, then shrink back", answer:"Rapid elasticity"},
    {text:"The office manager adds three new user licences in the admin centre in two minutes", answer:"On-demand self-service"},
    {text:"Thousands of small firms' mailboxes are hosted on the same physical servers", answer:"Resource pooling"},
    {text:"A dashboard shows the design team used 1.2 TB this month and HR used 40 GB", answer:"Measured service"},
    {text:"Extra virtual machines spin up within minutes when a render job starts", answer:"Rapid elasticity"},
    {text:"Staff check their calendar on a phone, a home laptop and a library PC", answer:"Ubiquitous access"}],
   explain:"Measured service is about metering and visibility of use; elasticity is about the capacity changing. A dashboard of usage per department is measured service, and is what makes chargeback possible."},
  {type:"classify", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A2 service models",
   q:"Classify each product or arrangement by service model.",
   options:["SaaS","PaaS","IaaS"],
   items:[
    {text:"Microsoft 365 with Teams, SharePoint and OneDrive", answer:"SaaS"},
    {text:"Amazon EC2 virtual machine running a Linux server you configure yourself", answer:"IaaS"},
    {text:"Google App Engine running your own Python web app", answer:"PaaS"},
    {text:"Dropbox Business shared folders", answer:"SaaS"},
    {text:"Azure Virtual Machines with a self-installed Nextcloud server", answer:"IaaS"},
    {text:"Azure App Service hosting your company intranet code", answer:"PaaS"},
    {text:"Salesforce customer relationship management", answer:"SaaS"},
    {text:"Amazon S3 raw object storage buckets used as a backup target", answer:"IaaS"}],
   explain:"Ask who manages the operating system. If you do, it is IaaS. If the provider does but you deploy code, it is PaaS. If you just log in and use it, it is SaaS. Nextcloud is SaaS-like for its users, but installing it on a VM you manage makes the hosting IaaS."},
  {type:"match", area:"A", ao:"A.M1", ref:"Learning aim A · A2 delivery models",
   q:"Match each organisation to the delivery model that fits it best.",
   options:["Public","Private","Hybrid","Community"],
   items:[
    {text:"A start-up with four staff and no IT budget that needs email and file sharing this week", answer:"Public"},
    {text:"A bank that must keep its core ledger on infrastructure only it controls, under regulator scrutiny", answer:"Private"},
    {text:"A manufacturer that keeps machine control on-site but moves email, HR and file sharing to Microsoft 365", answer:"Hybrid"},
    {text:"A group of NHS trusts sharing a platform designed around health data rules", answer:"Community"},
    {text:"An online ticket seller with a private cloud that bursts to a public provider when big concerts go on sale", answer:"Hybrid"},
    {text:"Several multi-academy trusts jointly procuring a shared, education-only cloud platform", answer:"Community"}],
   explain:"Cloud bursting is a hybrid pattern. Community clouds are defined by shared concerns among a closed group, not just by sharing."},
  {type:"match", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A5 legislation",
   q:"Match each situation to the legislation that most directly applies.",
   options:["Data Protection Act 2018 / UK GDPR","Computer Misuse Act 1990","Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988","Telecommunications lawful business practice / interception rules","Fraud Act 2006"],
   items:[
    {text:"A firm moves client records to a provider whose data centre is outside the UK without checking the transfer rules", answer:"Data Protection Act 2018 / UK GDPR"},
    {text:"An employee installs 20 copies of design software on cloud desktops using one single-user licence", answer:"Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988"},
    {text:"A student guesses an administrator's password and changes everyone's storage quotas", answer:"Computer Misuse Act 1990"},
    {text:"A company records and reviews staff Teams chats without having told staff it might do so", answer:"Telecommunications lawful business practice / interception rules"},
    {text:"Someone emails customers pretending to be the cloud provider to take payment for a fake storage upgrade", answer:"Fraud Act 2006"},
    {text:"A shared cloud folder of patient letters is accidentally made public, and nobody reports it", answer:"Data Protection Act 2018 / UK GDPR"}],
   explain:"The specification names telecommunications regulations covering lawful business practice and interception. In the UK, the 2000 Lawful Business Practice Regulations were replaced in 2018 by regulations under the Investigatory Powers Act 2016, but the principle that staff must be told about monitoring is the same."},
  {type:"classify", area:"A", ao:"A.M1", ref:"Learning aim A · A1/A4 benefits and drawbacks",
   q:"Is each point a benefit or a drawback of cloud adoption for an organisation like Harbour & Lane?",
   options:["Benefit","Drawback"],
   items:[
    {text:"No up-front capital cost for a replacement file server", answer:"Benefit"},
    {text:"If broadband fails in Durham, staff there lose access to live files", answer:"Drawback"},
    {text:"Automatic off-site copies replace the nightly USB backup", answer:"Benefit"},
    {text:"Upload of a 2 GB drawing on a 10 Mbps upstream link takes nearly half an hour", answer:"Drawback"},
    {text:"Surveyors can open drawings on site rather than printing them", answer:"Benefit"},
    {text:"Monthly subscription costs continue for as long as the service is used", answer:"Drawback"},
    {text:"The provider patches the platform, reducing reliance on a monthly contractor visit", answer:"Benefit"},
    {text:"Staff need training and some will resist changing how they save work", answer:"Drawback"}],
   explain:"At 10 Mbps upstream, 2 GB (about 16,000 megabits) takes around 1,600 seconds, so roughly 27 minutes. Quantifying a drawback like this is the kind of precise detail that lifts A.M1 and A.D1."},
  {type:"classify", area:"C", ao:"C.P5", ref:"Learning aim C · C1 testing",
   q:"Which type of test is each one?",
   options:["Functional","User","Performance","Stress","Security"],
   items:[
    {text:"Check that a file shared with view-only rights cannot be edited by the recipient", answer:"Security"},
    {text:"Time how long a 500 MB file takes to sync from Durham under normal working conditions", answer:"Performance"},
    {text:"Ask three designers to complete a set task and record where they got stuck", answer:"User"},
    {text:"Check that the version history restores the copy from yesterday afternoon", answer:"Functional"},
    {text:"Simulate all 38 staff uploading at once at 5pm on a Friday", answer:"Stress"},
    {text:"Attempt to sign in from a new device without completing multi-factor authentication", answer:"Security"},
    {text:"Check that creating a new project folder applies the correct template sub-folders", answer:"Functional"}],
   explain:"Permissions tests can be argued as functional, but because they check that access controls stop the wrong person, they belong under security. Always state the expected result before running any test."},
  {type:"match", area:"A", ao:"A.D1", ref:"Learning aim A · A4 dispute resolution",
   q:"Match each description to the dispute resolution approach.",
   options:["Statutory (court or regulator)","Mediation (voluntary)","Arbitration (binding)","Escalation path"],
   items:[
    {text:"A neutral third party helps both sides reach an agreement, but either can walk away", answer:"Mediation (voluntary)"},
    {text:"An independent arbitrator hears both sides and both are bound by the decision", answer:"Arbitration (binding)"},
    {text:"The contract sets out that a complaint goes to account manager, then service director, then board level", answer:"Escalation path"},
    {text:"The customer takes the provider to court for breach of contract, or complains to the ICO", answer:"Statutory (court or regulator)"}],
   explain:"Contracts usually set an escalation path first, then mediation or arbitration, with the courts as a last resort. Which country's courts apply is itself a jurisdiction question."},
  {type:"multi", area:"B", ao:"B.P4", ref:"Learning aim B · B3 development", marks:4,
   q:"Which FOUR of these are user management tasks listed in B3? Select all that apply.",
   options:["Creating accounts for new users","Choosing the guest OS image","Setting storage quotas","Installing the mobile app on staff phones","Setting privileges and access rights","Enforcing a password policy","Allocating host CPU to a virtual instance"],
   answers:[0,2,4,5],
   explain:"Guest OS images and host resource allocation are platform tasks; the mobile app is a cloud client. User management is accounts, passwords, quotas, privileges and access rights. A wrong selection cancels a correct one."}
  ]
},

/* ========================= LEVEL 3 ========================= */
{
  id:"L3", name:"Level 3", colour:"var(--l3)",
  title:"Level 3 · Apply to a Scenario",
  blurb:"Short written answers about one organisation. Every point must be tied to Harbour & Lane: a generic textbook answer earns very little here. This is the level of detail A.P1 and A.P2 need.",
  meta:["A.P1 · A.P2 · B.P3 · B.P4","3–4 marks per question","Self-marked"],
  recap:[
    {h:"The scenario",b:SCEN},
    {h:"Explain means link",
     b:"<p>An explanation has two halves: <span class='kw'>the point</span> and <span class='kw'>why it matters here</span>. Use connectives such as <em>which means</em>, <em>so</em>, <em>because</em>. A strong pattern: feature → how it works → effect on this organisation's staff, clients or costs.</p>"}
  ],
  questions:[
  {type:"written", area:"A", ao:"A.P1", ref:"Learning aim A · A3 current IT", marks:4, scenario:SCEN,
   q:"Explain how IT is currently deployed at Harbour & Lane, and two ways in which it does not meet the practice's needs.",
   teach:"A.P1 asks you to explain current deployment <b>and</b> how well it meets needs. Describe the hardware, software, connectivity and support, then judge them against what staff actually have to do.",
   model:["Harbour & Lane runs a traditional on-premises setup: one Windows Server 2016 file server in Newcastle holds all project files, an on-premises Exchange 2016 server handles email, Durham staff reach files over a VPN, and support comes from the office manager plus a monthly contractor visit.",
          "First, it does not support collaboration between sites or with surveyors. Durham staff working on 2 GB drawings over a slow VPN wait a long time to open and save files, and surveyors on site cannot see the current version, so they risk working from outdated drawings, which wastes time and could cause costly mistakes on a build.",
          "Second, the single server and USB backup are a resilience and security risk. If the cupboard server fails or the building floods, there is no automatic off-site copy beyond a USB stick in someone's house, which could be lost, unencrypted or a day out of date. Exchange 2016 reached the end of extended support in October 2025 and Windows Server 2016 follows in January 2027, so security patches stop, increasing the risk of a breach involving client personal data."],
   criteria:["Accurately described the current deployment (server, email, VPN, support)","Explained a collaboration or access weakness, linked to sites, surveyors or file size","Explained a resilience, backup or security weakness, linked to the single server or USB backup","Linked a weakness to an impact on the business (time, cost, risk to clients or data)"]},
  {type:"written", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A1 characteristics", marks:4, scenario:SCEN,
   q:"Explain two key characteristics of cloud computing that would benefit Harbour & Lane.",
   model:["Ubiquitous access would let surveyors open the current drawings on their tablets on site, and Durham staff work on the same files as Newcastle, without a VPN. This means everyone works from one version of the truth, reducing errors caused by out-of-date drawings and saving the time currently spent printing or emailing files.",
          "Rapid elasticity and on-demand self-service would let the office manager add storage or licences in minutes when the practice wins a large project or takes on contractors, and remove them afterwards. This means the practice no longer has to buy a bigger server in advance for peak demand, and pays only for what it uses, which addresses the partners' concerns about cost."],
   criteria:["Named a valid characteristic accurately","Explained how it would benefit Harbour & Lane specifically","Named a second valid characteristic accurately","Explained how the second characteristic would benefit Harbour & Lane specifically"]},
  {type:"written", area:"A", ao:"A.P2", ref:"Learning aim A · A2 service models", marks:3, scenario:SCEN,
   q:"Explain why a SaaS collaboration platform is likely to suit Harbour & Lane better than building its own solution on IaaS.",
   model:["With SaaS such as Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace, the provider manages the servers, operating system, application updates and security patching, whereas with IaaS the practice would have to install, patch and secure the guest operating system and the collaboration software itself.",
          "Harbour & Lane has no in-house IT specialist, only an office manager and a monthly contractor, so it does not have the skills or time to run IaaS virtual machines safely. An unpatched self-managed server is exactly the weakness it is trying to leave behind.",
          "SaaS also has a predictable per-user monthly price, includes email (replacing Exchange 2016) and has accessible clients that work with screen readers, which supports the visually impaired designer. IaaS would only make sense if the practice needed software that SaaS cannot provide."],
   criteria:["Explained the difference in who manages what (shared responsibility)","Linked the choice to Harbour & Lane's limited IT skills or support","Gave a further justified reason (cost, email replacement, accessibility, speed of deployment)"]},
  {type:"written", area:"A", ao:"A.P1", ref:"Learning aim A · A3 IT requirements", marks:3, scenario:SCEN,
   q:"Identify and explain three IT requirements that a consultant must investigate before recommending a cloud solution to Harbour & Lane.",
   model:["Staff working patterns and location: surveyors work on site two or three days a week and six staff work part-time or compressed hours, so the solution must offer reliable mobile and offline access, and support for sharing work at handover times.",
          "Staff accessibility needs: one designer uses a screen reader, so the consultant must check that the web and desktop clients meet accessibility standards and work with that assistive software. Choosing an inaccessible tool could also breach the Equality Act 2010.",
          "Legal and ethical requirements: the practice holds client personal data, so the consultant must check where the provider stores data, whether any transfer outside the UK is a restricted transfer under UK GDPR, and whether the CAD software licence allows cloud or remote use."],
   criteria:["Explained a staff working pattern or location requirement linked to the scenario","Explained an accessibility requirement linked to the screen reader user","Explained a legal or ethical requirement (data location, licensing, privacy) linked to the scenario"]},
  {type:"written", area:"A", ao:"A.M1", ref:"Learning aim A · A4 constraints", marks:3, scenario:SCEN,
   q:"Explain how bandwidth could constrain cloud adoption at the Durham office, and suggest one way to reduce the problem.",
   teach:"Show the arithmetic. Megabits per second (Mb) is not the same as megabytes (MB): 1 byte is 8 bits. 2 GB is about 16,000 megabits.",
   model:["Durham has only 10 Mbps upload. A 2 GB drawing is about 16,000 megabits, so uploading it takes roughly 1,600 seconds, which is around 27 minutes, and the link is shared with every other user in the office. If several designers save large files at once, the connection will saturate and video calls and other work will slow down.",
          "The practice could use a sync client with differential sync, which uploads only the changed blocks of a file rather than the whole file each time, and schedule large initial uploads overnight.",
          "It could also upgrade Durham to a full-fibre business connection with symmetric speeds, and add a 4G or 5G backup link so that the office is not cut off if the main line fails."],
   criteria:["Identified the low upstream bandwidth as the constraint","Quantified or clearly explained the effect on large CAD files and shared users","Suggested a realistic, justified mitigation"]},
  {type:"written", area:"B", ao:"B.P4", ref:"Learning aim B · B3 user management", marks:4, scenario:SCEN,
   q:"Explain how you would set up user management in the new collaboration solution for Harbour & Lane.",
   model:["I would create groups by role (Partners, Designers, Surveyors, Admin, External clients) and assign access rights to groups rather than individuals, so that adding a new starter takes one step and permissions stay consistent.",
          "Privileges would follow least privilege: only the office manager and the external contractor would hold administrator rights, with separate admin accounts; designers would have edit rights to project sites; clients would get view-only guest access to a single project folder, with links that expire.",
          "I would set storage quotas per user for personal space (for example 100 GB) with shared project libraries holding the large CAD files, so storage use can be monitored and planned.",
          "Passwords would follow NCSC guidance: long passphrases rather than forced frequent changes, with multi-factor authentication required for every account, especially admins and anyone signing in from site tablets."],
   criteria:["Used role-based groups for access rights","Applied least privilege, including separate or limited admin accounts","Set storage quotas in a way suited to the scenario","Specified a password and MFA policy"]}
  ]
},

/* ========================= LEVEL 4 ========================= */
{
  id:"L4", name:"Level 4", colour:"var(--l4)",
  title:"Level 4 · Analyse",
  blurb:"Six-mark answers that weigh things up. Compare options point by point, use real evidence and reach a conclusion. This is the Merit (A.M1) and early Distinction (A.D1) standard.",
  meta:["A.M1 · A.D1","6 marks per question","Self-marked"],
  recap:[
    {h:"Compare versus analyse",
     b:"<p><span class='kw'>Compare (Merit)</span>: set out the similarities and differences between options, point by point. The guidance says this may be unbalanced. <span class='kw'>Analyse (Distinction)</span>: break each impact into its causes and effects, show how factors interact, weigh benefits against drawbacks for this specific organisation, and justify why one option is preferable.</p>"},
    {h:"Real cases to draw on",
     b:"<ul><li><span class='kw'>AWS, 20 October 2025</span>: a 14-hour outage in the US-East-1 region disrupted services worldwide; in the UK, Lloyds Banking Group brands and HMRC were affected.</li><li><span class='kw'>Azure Front Door, 29 October 2025</span>: a faulty configuration change caused around eight hours of disruption, including to Microsoft 365.</li><li><span class='kw'>OVHcloud, March 2021</span>: a fire at the Strasbourg campus destroyed one data centre; about 65,000 customers were affected and some lost data permanently because their backups were on the same site.</li><li><span class='kw'>Microsoft France, June 2025</span>: under oath to a French Senate inquiry, Microsoft's legal director said he could not guarantee that EU-hosted data would never be handed to US authorities.</li><li><span class='kw'>CMA, July 2025</span>: the UK competition regulator found AWS and Microsoft each held 30–40% of UK IaaS supply, with egress fees and licensing making it harder to switch provider.</li></ul>"}
  ],
  questions:[
  {type:"written", area:"A", ao:"A.M1", ref:"Learning aim A · A2 delivery models", marks:6, scenario:SCEN,
   q:"Compare a fully public cloud solution with a hybrid solution for Harbour & Lane.",
   model:["Both options would replace the cupboard file server for day-to-day collaboration and give surveyors and Durham staff ubiquitous access. In a fully public solution, all files and email move to a SaaS platform such as Microsoft 365; in a hybrid solution, current projects move to the cloud but a local server or NAS in Newcastle keeps a synchronised copy of large CAD files and archived projects.",
          "On cost, public cloud has the lowest up-front cost and a predictable per-user subscription, whereas hybrid keeps some capital spending on local hardware and its maintenance, so it costs more overall but may reduce subscription storage charges for the 2 GB files.",
          "On performance, hybrid gives Newcastle designers LAN-speed access to big files and protects them from a broadband outage, whereas public cloud makes every user dependent on upload speed, which is a real problem for Durham's 10 Mbps link.",
          "On management and security, public cloud is simpler: the provider patches everything and there is one system to secure. Hybrid needs someone to look after the local server and the sync between the two, which the practice's thin IT support may struggle with, so the local server could again become the unpatched weak point.",
          "Overall, public cloud fits the practice's lack of IT staff and its cost concerns better, while hybrid fits its large-file and resilience concerns better."],
   criteria:["Set out what each option would look like for the practice","Compared on cost, with a clear difference","Compared on performance or bandwidth, with a clear difference","Compared on management, skills or security, with a clear difference","Used scenario detail (Durham link, file sizes, IT support) throughout","Summarised which needs each option meets best"]},
  {type:"written", area:"A", ao:"A.D1", ref:"Learning aim A · A4 impact and risk", marks:6,
   scenario:"<p>On 20 October 2025, Amazon Web Services' US-East-1 region in Northern Virginia suffered an outage that lasted around 14 hours. It began with DNS problems affecting the DynamoDB database service and spread to EC2 virtual machines and network load balancers. In the UK, Lloyds, Halifax and Bank of Scotland customers, HMRC and many apps such as Snapchat, Signal, Slack and Ring doorbells were disrupted.</p>",
   q:"Analyse what the October 2025 AWS outage shows about the risks for organisations that depend on a third-party cloud provider, and how those risks can be reduced.",
   model:["The outage shows that resource pooling creates concentration risk. Thousands of organisations shared the same region and the same internal services, so one fault in DNS for a single database service cascaded into failures for banks, a government department and consumer apps at the same moment. The efficiency that makes public cloud cheap is the same thing that turns one fault into a mass outage.",
          "It also shows that organisations lose control when the provider owns the infrastructure. A UK bank cannot repair a data centre in Virginia; it can only wait, communicate with customers and absorb reputational damage. Many UK-facing services had dependencies on a US region that their own customers would never have known about, which is a hidden risk in the architecture.",
